<section class="testimonial-area  py-0 mt-5" style="background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(77, 79, 95, 1), rgba(77, 79, 95, 1)) !important;">
    <div class="container py-5">
        <div class="testimonial-wrapper-two">
            <div class="row no-gutters">
                <div class="col-lg-6 d-flex justify-content-center align-items-center">
                    <img src="/webassets/images/elements/steo-10.jpg" alt="img" style="border: 2px solid white; ">
                </div>
                <div class="col-lg-6 pl-lg-3 d-flex align-items-center ">
                    <div class="testimonial-content-box bg_cover wow fadeInRight justify-content-center align-items-center d-flex flex-column" style="visibility: visible; animation-name: fadeInRight; background-image: none !important;">
                        <div class="section-title section-title-left pb-1" style="text-align: start !important;">
                            <h4 style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 23px;color: white;">What is a Studmale and what does he need?</h4>
                            <p class="mt-2" style="color: white;">A stud refers to a male cat that is retained for breeding purposes. He possesses desirable physical and behavioral traits that breeders wish to pass on to the next generation of their kittens. The term “stud” highlights the cat’s role in breeding, as he is used to providing litters. Typically, a stud is selected based on various attributes, including:</p>
                        </div>
                        <p style="color: white; display: flex;"> <i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Physical Attributes: A stud male should ideally represent the best physical qualities of his breed. This includes the size of the cat and his offspring, coat quality and looks, and physical characteristics.</p>
                        <p style="color: white; display: flex;" class="mt-2"> <i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right mr-2"></i> Health: It’s essential that a Stud male is free from genetic disorders or diseases that can be passed on to his offspring. Regular health checks like FIV/FeLV tests and certain health clearances might be mandatory in many formal breeding settings. Some breeds ask specific checks, like HCM and PKD for example. You can ask your association what kind of tests are required for your breed.</p>

                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

        </div>
    </div>

</section>
